          Fragment:md
            ## Basics

            nmbl is an **indentation-based** shorthand for HTML -
            indentation level determines nesting — there are no closing tags. A
            tag name starts a line; children go on indented lines beneath it.

            ```nmbl
            nav
              ul
                li Home
                li About
            ```

            ### `#id` and `.class` shorthand

            Use CSS-selector shorthand glued directly to the tag: `#id` for the id,
            `.class` for classes (repeat `.` for multiple classes). A selector with **no tag
            name** implies a `div`.

            ```nmbl no-toggle
            nav#main-nav.sticky   → <nav id="main-nav" class="sticky">
            .foo.bar              → <div class="foo bar">
            ```

            ### HTML Tag / Components

            Tag is passed through directly, whether it's a standard HTML tag or a component.
            It is passed through untouched — your framework resolves it.

            ```nmbl
            button         // standard HTML tag
            MyFancyButton  // custom component
            ```

            ## Attributes

            Attributes go inside parens **glued** to the tag head, separated by
            whitespace; the parens may span multiple lines (newlines inside them
            aren't significant). Values can be quoted strings, bare values
            (`colspan=2`), or a raw `{expression}` hole; a valueless name is a
            boolean attribute.

            ```nmbl
            a.btn(href="/x" target="_blank") Go
            input(type="email" required)
            td(colspan=2) Cell
            button(onClick={() => save()}) Save
            input(
              type="email"
              name="email"
              required
            )
            ```

            ### Bindings

            Most of the time you just write what's **native** to your framework —
            Vue's `:src="…"` and `v-if`, or the `{expression}` holes Svelte, Astro,
            and JSX use — and nmbl passes it straight through. The only bit of
            standardization is the bound `:` shorthand: it compiles to each host's
            native binding (`:attr="…"` on Vue/HTML, `attr={…}` on the JSX-likes), so
            the same markup works across every target:

            ```
            :src="avatar"      →  :src="avatar"        ·  src={avatar}
            :src=`/a/${id}`    →  :src="`/a/${id}`"    ·  src={`/a/${id}`}
            ```

            Everything else is left as-is rather than rewritten:

            - A raw `{expression}` hole is native to **Svelte / Astro / JSX** and
              passes straight through. In **Vue** it isn't a binding — `src={avatar}`
              would become the literal string `{avatar}` — so the Vue target raises a
              compile error pointing you to `:src="…"` (a prop) or `@event` (a
              handler) rather than emit something broken.
            - A bare ``attr=`…` `` (no `:`) is a **literal** string; its backticks
              aren't evaluated. Bind it (``:attr=`…` ``) to make it dynamic.
            - A valueless `:name` is shorthand for `:name="name"`.

            In Vue, the host's own attribute directives — `v-model`, `@click.stop`,
            `:prop`, `#slot` — pass straight through untouched, so write them however
            you like.

            ### Class merging

            A static class (`.card`) plus a dynamic `:class` merges into one binding
            on Svelte / Astro / JSX, while Vue keeps them separate (it merges classes
            itself):

            ```nmbl
            div.card(:class={active})
            ```

            - **svelte / astro** → <code>class={`card ${(active) ?? ''}`}</code>
            - **jsx (React)** → <code>className={`card ${(active) ?? ''}`}</code>
            - **vue** → `class="card" :class={active}`

            ### React aliases

            For the React JSX target, nmbl renames the attributes React expects:
            `class` → `className` and `for` → `htmlFor`. Solid and Qwik keep the
            native names.

            ## Text and raw HTML

            Text can follow the tag head on the same line. For standalone or
            multi-line text, prefix the line with a **pipe** (`|`). Pipes also let
            you mix text with inline child elements — each `|` line is a text node
            sitting alongside its element siblings:

            ```nmbl
            p Inline text after the tag head

            p
              | Read the
              a(href="/docs") docs
              | to learn more.
            ```

            That compiles to one paragraph with the link inline:
            `<p>Read the <a href="/docs">docs</a> to learn more.</p>`.

            ### Pipe text vs. a content block

            Two different jobs. Use `|` when text is **interleaved** with elements
            (above). When you want the element's **entire** body treated as raw text
            — no tags parsed at all — use a content block (a glued trailing `:`,
            covered [below](#content-blocks)). Inside a content block, an
            element-looking line stays literal text:

            ```nmbl
            p
              | mix text and
              em elements              ← <em>elements</em>

            p:
              text and em elements     ← all literal; <em> is NOT parsed
            ```

            ### Preserving whitespace

            nmbl trims the whitespace around each text fragment, and editors strip
            trailing spaces on save — so an intentional space *between* inline
            siblings is easily lost. Type the space, then end the line with `\` to
            shield it from the editor:

            ```nmbl
            p
              | click \
              a(href="/post/1") here \
              | to see the details
            ```

            Each `\` keeps the space before the next sibling, so the words and the
            link stay separated — `click here to see the details` — instead of
            colliding into `clickhereto see…`. The `\` only **protects** the
            whitespace you already typed and is itself dropped — it never invents a
            space (a `\` with no space before it is a no-op), and it preserves the
            spaces *verbatim*, count and all:

            ```nmbl no-toggle
            span gap of three.   \   → <span>gap of three.   </span>
            span no space.\          → <span>no space.</span>
            ```

            This works on **any** text, not just `|` lines — inline text after a tag
            head, as above, included.

            This round-trips: the decompiler (HTML → nmbl) emits the `\` back when a
            text node carries a significant trailing space, so converting markup in
            and out preserves the gap.

            ### Raw HTML

            Raw `<…>` tags pass through **verbatim** — both **inline** inside text
            (a stray `<b>` or `<a>`) and as **full lines** (a pasted SVG, the
            doctype). nmbl never escapes them, so you mix markup right where you need
            it:

            ```nmbl
            p Built with <b>nmbl</b> — read the <a href="/docs">docs</a>.

            button.copy(type="button" aria-label="Copy code")
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true">
              <rect x="9" y="9" width="11" height="11" rx="2"/>
              <path d="M5 15V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h8"/>
              </svg>
              | Copy
            ```

            Converting pasted markup into nmbl is usually the right move — that's
            the point of the notation, and the decompiler will do it for you. **SVG
            is the exception: leave it as raw HTML.** An SVG is XML that other
            programs read — design tools, optimizers like SVGO, anything expecting a
            `.svg` file. Kept verbatim, it copies straight back out into those tools
            and the edited version pastes straight back in; converted to nmbl, every
            round trip needs a translation step in both directions.
            One rule: every line of a raw block must sit at the **same** indentation
            (note the `<svg>`, `<rect>`, `<path>`, and `</svg>` lines above are all
            flush); a line indented deeper than the one above it is a parse error, so
            flatten the block's internal indentation.

            ## Block expansion

            Nest a single child inline with `>` (spaces on both sides). Great for
            link lists and wrappers.

            ```nmbl no-toggle
            li > a(href="/") Home   → <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
            ```

            The spaces matter: `li > a` is expansion, but a `>` elsewhere in inline
            text passes through as a literal character.

            ## Comments

            Comments come in a **stripped** tier (dev notes — gone from the output)
            and a **rendered** tier (kept as a real HTML comment). Add `!` to render.

            At the element level, use line comments — `//` to strip, `//!` to
            render:

            ```nmbl
            // stripped from output — never shipped
            //! rendered as <!-- an HTML comment -->
            p Visible text
            ```

            For a multi-line rendered comment, the block form `/*! … */` works too:

            ```nmbl
            /*!
              shipped as an HTML comment,
              across several lines
            */
            ```

            ### Commenting out attributes and props

            Because attributes live on their own lines inside the parens, you can
            comment **a single attribute or component prop** out — something HTML has
            no way to express at all, and JSX only manages with a `{/* … */}`
            wrapper:

            ```nmbl
            button(
              type="submit"
              // disabled
              aria-label="Save changes"
            ) Save
            ```

            Component props work exactly the same way, which makes toggling one
            prop off a one-character edit instead of a delete-and-retype:

            ```nmbl
            PricingCard(
              tier="pro"
              price={29}
              // badge="Most popular"
            )
            ```

            A `//` runs to the end of its line, so the closing `)` has to sit on a
            later line. To comment something out **mid-line**, use the block form
            `/* … */` instead:

            ```nmbl
            button(type="submit" /* disabled */) Save
            ```

            > The rendered forms (`//!`, `/*! … */`) are element-level only — inside
            > parens they're a parse error. An attribute can be commented out, but
            > never shipped as an HTML comment.

            ## Content blocks

            A **glued** trailing `:` (or `:mode`) turns every more-indented line into
            **raw text** — not parsed as nmbl. Built-in uses are `script:` and
            `style:`; a named mode like `article:md` runs the body through a
            registered filter.

            ```nmbl
            script:
              const x = 1 < 2;   // not parsed as nmbl
            style:
              .a { color: red }
            ```

            ## Markdown sections

            Append `:md` to any element and its body becomes Markdown, rendered at
            build time. It works in **every** integration — reach for it instead of
            stacks of inline `<h3>` / `<p>` / `<code>` in prose-heavy sections.

            ```nmbl
            .prose:md
              ### A heading

              A paragraph with [links](/x), `code`, and **bold**. Blank lines
              separate paragraphs, and raw inline HTML works too.
            ```

            - **Astro** routes it through your project's *own* Markdown pipeline — so
              fenced code blocks with syntax highlighting (` ``` `), GFM tables, and any
              remark/rehype plugins you've added (e.g. `:::aside` directives) all work
              exactly as they do in a `.md` file.
            - **Vue / Svelte / `.nmbl` files / JSX templates** use a built-in
              CommonMark + GFM renderer; override it with a custom `md` filter.
            - The rendered HTML is spliced back into the host template, so `{expr}` /
              `{{ expr }}` interpolation and `<Component />` tags still work inside the
              prose (MDX-like). Braces inside code spans/fences are auto-escaped.

            ### Bare `:md`

            With no tag in front, a `:md` block renders its Markdown straight into the
            surrounding output — no wrapper element:

            ```nmbl
            :md
              ## Standalone prose

              Rendered directly into the page.
            ```

            The exception is the **JSX** target: there a `:md` body compiles to
            `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, which needs a host element — so attach it
            (`div:md`). A bare `:md` is a compile error under `jsx`.

            ## Control flow

            One notation compiles to each host's **native** control flow — write it
            once, get idiomatic output everywhere. Conditions and loop sources are
            plain host-JS, passed through verbatim. In Vue the `@`-blocks are
            entirely optional: `v-if` / `v-for` pass straight through, so reach for
            them or the `@`-sugar, whichever you prefer.

            ```nmbl
            @if(loggedIn)
              p Welcome back, {user.name}
            @elseif(guest)
              a(href="/signup") Sign up
            @else
              a(href="/login") Log in

            ul
              @each(item, i of items :key="item.id")
                li {i + 1}. {item.label}
            ```

            The loop accepts a few forms — the index and `:key` are optional, and
            Svelte's `as` form is recognized too (prefer the `of` form):

            ```nmbl
            @each(item of items)                        ← value only
            @each(item, i of items)                     ← value + index
            @each(item of items :key="item.id")         ← keyed
            @each(items as item, i (item.id))           ← Svelte-style, also accepted
            ```

            Here is what each target emits:

            | Target | Output |
            |---|---|
            | `svelte` | `{#if}…{:else}…{/if}`, `{#each items as item, i (key)}`; plus `@await` / `@key` / `@snippet` |
            | `vue` | `<template v-if="…">`, `<template v-for="… of …" :key="…">` |
            | `astro` / `jsx` | `{cond ? (…) : (…)}`, `{items.map((item, i) => (…))}`; jsx puts `key={…}` on the loop's single root |
            | `html` | no `@`-blocks (compile error) |

            > nmbl **errors loudly** rather than emitting something wrong: `@await` /
            > `@key` / `@snippet` outside Svelte, `{@html}` in Vue (use a `v-html`
            > attribute), or a multi-root `@each` body with `:key` in JSX are all hard
            > compile errors with hints.

            ## Interpolation

            Interpolation is always the **host's** syntax — nmbl never parses it, it
            just passes it through: `{{ msg }}` in Vue, `{expr}` in Svelte and Astro,
            and inside `nmbl`…`` JSX literals either `${hole}` holes or plain `{expr}`
            text.

            ## Pitfalls

            These cause real compile errors — nmbl reports them with exact source
            positions, so trust the messages. The most common are **gluing**
            mistakes: a stray space turns structure into plain text.

            ```nmbl
            a(href="/")      ✓  attribute list binds to the tag
            a (href="/")     ✗  the space makes (href="/") literal text
            div.card         ✓  selector glued to the tag
            div .card        ✗  the space makes .card literal text
            li > a           ✓  block expansion — spaces around > are required
            ```

            A few structural rules round it out:

            - **A glued trailing `:` opens a content block** — a single word + `:` on
              its own line captures the lines below it as raw text.
            - **Text lines need a `|`** (or must start with `<`); a bare text line
              raises a diagnostic.
            - **Don't mix tabs and spaces** — pick 2-space indentation.

            When in doubt, paste into the playground and watch it compile live.

          .next-steps
            h2 Next Steps
            .next-links
              a.next-link(href="/playground")
                span Try the Playground
                <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                <polyline points="9 18 15 12 9 6"></polyline>
                </svg>
              a.next-link(href="/guides")
